LESSONS_P8 = [
L("Chapter 1 — Advanced buying: scope and structure", "11", "<p>This module completes the Procurement track: the instruments that manage supplier performance and repeat supply, the configuration register that governs the buying documents, and the consolidated calendar that assembles every read modules 1-7 defined. On completing this module you will be able to: build and read supplier scorecards with weighted criteria from document data; run the standings and their actions; conduct relationship reviews from measured evidence; operate buying-side blanket orders and distinguish them from rate arrangements; state the subcontracting flow and when it applies; maintain Buying Settings as control-register rows; and run the procurement function on its consolidated calendar.</p><p><b>Where this module sits.</b> Modules 1-7 built the transaction machinery: the supplier register, the item's buying side, demand, the quotation cycle, the order, the receipt, and the match. Their documents accumulated measured facts at every step — confirmed dates against arrival dates, ordered quantities against counted quantities, agreed rates against claimed rates, claims against resolutions. This module is where those facts become management: <b>the supplier relationship is managed from what the documents measured, and the conversations that shape next year's supply are conducted from this year's records.</b></p><p><b>The module's instruments.</b></p><p>1. <b>Supplier scorecards</b> (Chapters 2-3) — the weighted, periodic measurement of each supplier from document data, with standings that carry configured consequences.<br>2. <b>Relationship reviews</b> (Chapter 4) — the scheduled conversations, tiered by supplier importance, agenda'd from the scorecard and the files.<br>3. <b>Blanket orders</b> (Chapter 5) — committed-quantity agreements for repeat supply, called off as needed.<br>4. <b>Subcontracting</b> (Chapter 6) — supplying materials to a vendor and receiving finished goods, where the site's operations use it.<br>5. <b>The control register and the calendar</b> (Chapter 7) — the Buying Settings rows and the assembled reads.</p><p><b>Custody.</b> The procurement function maintains scorecards and runs the calendar; review outcomes minute per the standing record rules; scorecard criteria and weights are policy decisions changed by decision with dates; and the standing rule: <b>performance is measured from documents, standings carry stated consequences, and no supplier conversation of consequence happens without the file open.</b></p><p><b>Module contents.</b> Chapter 2: scorecard structure. Chapter 3: standings and actions. Chapter 4: relationship reviews. Chapter 5: blanket orders. Chapter 6: subcontracting. Chapter 7: the control register and the calendar. Chapter 8: worked example. Chapter 9: review, the module rule, and the track close.</p>"),
L("Chapter 2 — Supplier scorecards: structure and weights", "12", "<p>The scorecard measures each supplier per period from document data. This chapter is its construction.</p><p><b>1. The Supplier Scorecard doctype.</b> Open Supplier Scorecard (awesome bar) &gt; New, per supplier: the evaluation period (monthly or quarterly per the policy), the weighted criteria, and the standings ladder (Chapter 3). The system evaluates each period automatically from the variables below, and the period scores accumulate into the supplier's running standing.</p><p><b>2. The criteria and the standing weights.</b> Each criterion carries a weight; the weights sum to one hundred, and the standing policy sets them:</p><p>1. <b>Price competitiveness — weight 40.</b> The supplier's rates against the market evidence: the quotation cycles' comparisons (module 4), the trend read's movements against notice and market reference (module 2), and rate fidelity at the match (module 7). The heaviest weight because price is the largest lever on the landed cost the business sells against.<br>2. <b>Delivery performance — weight 35.</b> On-time-in-full from the receiving records (module 6): arrival dates against confirmed dates per drop, and quantity fidelity — short receipts and over-receipts against ordered rows. Measured per drop precisely because module 5's split rows made each drop its own commitment.<br>3. <b>Quality — weight 25.</b> The condition record: rejection rates by cause from the receipts, inspection failures from the quarantine lane, claim frequency and resolution conduct from the claims file.</p><p><b>3. The variables are document facts.</b> Each criterion computes from the fields the track's documents captured: the scorecard is credible precisely because its inputs were recorded at the dock, on the orders, and in the match — not recalled at review time. A scorecard fed by impressions measures the loudest recent event; a scorecard fed by documents measures the year. Where a variable cannot compute from documents for a period (a supplier with no orders in the window), the period is marked as unmeasured rather than scored by assumption, and the standing carries forward on the measured periods.</p><p><b>4. Weight governance.</b> The 40/35/25 structure is the standing policy for the core goods classes; classes may vary by decision (essential lines weighting delivery higher, per module 4's award criteria mirror), and any change is a dated policy decision recorded with the scorecard configuration — the same fields-move-by-decision rule as every register in the programme. The weights are reviewed annually with the policy, not adjusted to move a particular supplier's score.</p>"),
L("Chapter 3 — Standings and actions", "12", "<p>Scores convert to standings, and standings carry consequences. This chapter is the ladder and its operation.</p><p><b>1. The standings ladder.</b> The scorecard configuration maps score bands to standings, and each standing carries configured actions:</p><p>1. <b>The healthy band</b> — no restrictions: the relationship operates normally, and the score's trend is the review's material.<br>2. <b>The warning band</b> — the score triggers the flag: the supplier's next cycle participation and open orders continue, and the warning routes to the relationship review's agenda with the criterion that moved — a warning is a scheduled conversation, not a quiet note.<br>3. <b>The restriction bands</b> — the configured gates: prevent new Requests for Quotation, prevent new Purchase Orders — applied by the system at the configured standing. A supplier the business will not order from is a standing with a reason on file, not an informal understanding one buyer holds.</p><p><b>2. The actions are stated in advance.</b> The ladder's structure means consequences are policy, applied evenly: the supplier at the restriction band is there by measured score, the standing is visible on their record, and the route back — the improvement the next periods must show — is arithmetic rather than persuasion. The evenness matters commercially: a supplier base that knows performance is measured and consequences are automatic negotiates differently from one that knows exceptions are available to whoever asks.</p><p><b>3. Overrides.</b> The configured gate can be overridden where the policy permits — the sole-source supplier whose restriction would stop the business — by the named role, with the reason recorded and the item routed to the review: an override is a documented risk acceptance, and repeated overrides for one supplier are the concentration finding (module 1) restated as a performance finding.</p><p><b>4. The scorecard feeds the machinery.</b></p><p>1. <b>The comparison worksheet</b> (module 4) carries the scorecard figures in its performance columns — the award weighs measured history.<br>2. <b>The review agenda</b> (Chapter 4) opens with the standing and its movement.<br>3. <b>The planning fields</b> (module 2) draw lead-time reality from the delivery record.<br>4. <b>The concentration and risk reads</b> (module 1) read standings across the base: a critical class whose suppliers cluster in warning bands is a supply risk stated in advance.</p><p><b>5. The measured-conversation rule.</b> Every performance conversation with a supplier cites the periods, the criteria, and the documents behind them. The rule works in both directions: the supplier who disputes a figure is answered from the receipts and the signed notes — and where the supplier is right, the correction is made and the score recomputes, because the scorecard's authority rests on its accuracy.</p>"),
L("Chapter 4 — Relationship reviews", "12", "<p>The scheduled conversations that manage the supplier base. This chapter is the cadence, the agenda, and the record.</p><p><b>1. The tiering.</b> Reviews scale by supplier importance, per the policy:</p><p>1. <b>The critical tier</b> — the concentration suppliers and sole sources (module 1's risk read names them): quarterly reviews, attended at the relationship level.<br>2. <b>The core tier</b> — the standing arrangement suppliers: semi-annual reviews.<br>3. <b>The transactional tier</b> — annual file reviews, meeting only where the scorecard or the claims file raises cause.</p><p><b>2. The agenda, from the files.</b> The review is prepared, not improvised — the agenda assembles from the track's records:</p><p>1. <b>The scorecard</b> — the standing, the period trend, the criterion movements (Chapter 3).<br>2. <b>The claims and exceptions file</b> — open claims by age, the quarter's matching exceptions by type (module 7's pattern), resolution conduct both ways.<br>3. <b>Payment performance, both directions</b> — the supplier's delivery against the business's payment timeliness (module 8 reads the module 7 aging honestly: the business's own overdue lines are on this agenda too, because the relationship's terms credibility is negotiated capital).<br>4. <b>The commercial pipeline</b> — arrangement validity and the re-quote calendar (module 4), volume trajectory against bands, the concentration position, and the coming year's structure.</p><p><b>3. The conduct.</b> The measured-conversation rule governs: figures cited from documents, disputes resolved against records, and the improvement asks specific — the criterion, the target, the period. The review is also where the supplier's asks are heard on the same standard: their statement reconciliation items, their payment experience, their forecast needs — the file is opened in both directions because supply relationships that survive price cycles are built on mutual records, not mutual impressions.</p><p><b>4. The outcome minute.</b> One page per review, filed with the supplier's record: the standing and trend as discussed, the commitments made each way (the improvement targets, the payment or forecast undertakings), the commercial decisions (renewal intentions, band adjustments routed to the module 4 governance), and the date of the next review. The minute is the continuity instrument — the next review opens by reading the last one's commitments against what the documents then measured — and the annual compilation of minutes per tier files with the procurement function's records for the owner's yearly read of the supply base.</p>"),
L("Chapter 5 — Blanket orders", "12", "<p>Repeat supply can commit in advance. This chapter is the buying-side blanket order and its boundaries.</p><p><b>1. The instrument.</b> The blanket order records a committed quantity of an item from a supplier at an agreed rate over a period — the buying mirror of the selling side's instrument. Individual Purchase Orders then <b>call off</b> against it: each call-off draws the blanket's rate, consumes its quantity, and runs the ordinary machinery (receipt, match, payment) as any order does.</p><p><b>2. Blanket order versus arrangement.</b> The distinction from module 4's rate arrangements, stated precisely:</p><p>1. <b>The arrangement</b> holds agreed rates (or bands) with <b>no quantity commitment</b> — the business buys what it needs at the agreed price; the supplier holds the price for the validity.<br>2. <b>The blanket order</b> adds the <b>quantity commitment</b> — the business commits to the volume, and the rate reflects that commitment. The commitment is the price's consideration: a blanket rate is typically inside the arrangement band precisely because the supplier can plan production against committed volume.<br>3. <b>The choice is a decision</b> — commit volume where demand is stable and the rate improvement pays for the flexibility surrendered; hold to arrangements where demand varies. The consolidation history (module 3's worksheets across periods) is the evidence the choice reads.</p><p><b>3. The mechanics.</b> The blanket order records supplier, item, committed quantity, rate, and period; call-off orders reference it and consume it; the remaining balance is visible on the record. The call-offs are ordinary orders in every other respect — scheduled per row, received at the dock, matched at the invoice — and the blanket's rate governs them as the arrangement's rate governs its purchases.</p><p><b>4. The consumption read — monthly.</b> Committed quantity against called-off quantity against period elapsed: the trajectory states whether the commitment will be met. Under-consumption approaching period end is a commercial conversation held early — the shortfall's treatment per the agreement's terms (carry-over, rate adjustment, or settlement) is negotiated from the trajectory, not discovered at expiry. Over-consumption ahead of schedule is the renewal conversation arriving early, with the volume evidence in hand.</p><p><b>5. Expiry is a negotiation, never a rollover.</b> The blanket's end date enters the re-quote calendar (module 4): renewal re-prices against the period's actual volumes, the scorecard's measured performance, and the market's movement — the same rule as every validity in the track, because a commitment renewed by inattention is a rate the market stopped testing.</p>"),
L("Chapter 6 — Subcontracting", "12", "<p>Some operations send materials out and receive finished goods back. This chapter is the subcontracting flow.</p><p><b>1. What subcontracting is.</b> The business supplies raw materials to a vendor; the vendor processes them; the business receives finished goods and pays a processing charge. The structure appears in retail-adjacent operations as repacking (bulk into retail packs), kitting and bundling at a co-packer, labelling and compliance marking, and light assembly — the vendor's service transforms stock the business already owns.</p><p><b>2. The document flow.</b></p><p>1. <b>The subcontracting order</b> — the Purchase Order flagged for subcontracting: the finished item, its quantity, and the <b>service rate</b> (the processing charge per unit) — the vendor is being bought a service, not goods.<br>2. <b>The materials definition</b> — the finished item's bill of materials states what raw materials each unit consumes: the components, their quantities, the expected consumption.<br>3. <b>The material transfer</b> — the raw materials move to the <b>subcontractor's warehouse</b>: a warehouse in the business's own tree (module 1 of the Inventory track) representing stock physically at the vendor — still the business's stock, on the business's books, visible to every stock read. The transfer documents what was sent, with identity where controlled.<br>4. <b>The receipt</b> — finished goods receive against the order per module 6's full discipline: counted, condition-split, identified. Posting consumes the raw materials from the subcontractor warehouse per the materials definition and brings the finished goods into stock.<br>5. <b>The invoice and match</b> — the vendor invoices the service charge; the match runs service-rate against order per module 7.</p><p><b>3. The valuation.</b> The finished item's cost assembles from its parts: the consumed materials at their carried valuation plus the service charge — the landed-thinking rule (module 2) applied to processing: the shelf cost of the repacked unit includes what it cost to have it repacked.</p><p><b>4. The reconciliation reads.</b> The subcontractor warehouse reconciles like any warehouse — the stock the books say is at the vendor against the vendor's own statement of holdings, per the counting rules — and the consumption reconciles per order: materials sent against materials consumed against finished goods received, with variances (process loss beyond the definition's allowance, unreturned excess) worked as claims per the standing procedures. Stock at vendors is stock at risk in a specific way: it is inventory the business cannot see daily, and the reconciliation cadence is the control.</p>"),
L("Chapter 7 — The control register and the procurement calendar", "13", "<p>The buying documents run under configured controls, and the function runs on a calendar. This chapter closes both.</p><p><b>1. Buying Settings as control-register rows.</b> Each setting is a policy decision with an owner and a reason, per the standing control-register pattern:</p><p>1. <b>Purchase Order required</b> — receipts and invoices require an order reference: the configuration that enforces module 3's standing rule (no purchase without its demand and commitment) and gives the match its first document. The exception role, where the site permits direct invoicing for defined classes (utilities, statutory payments), is named with its classes.<br>2. <b>Purchase Receipt required</b> — stock invoices require a receipt: the second document's guarantee, and the reason billed-not-received is an exception rather than a norm.<br>3. <b>Maintain same rate through the cycle</b> — the order's rate carries to receipt and invoice: the configuration behind module 6's untouched-rate rule and module 7's rate match, with variances routed to exceptions rather than absorbed.<br>4. <b>Over-receipt tolerance</b> — the dock's admission band (module 6), low, with the exception role named.<br>5. <b>Supplier-item and identity defaults</b> — the register behaviours the site standardises: naming series, default groups, the mandatory fields the gate enforces (module 1).</p><p><b>2. The settings read — quarterly.</b> The register sampled against configuration; changes since last read reconciled to their decisions; the drift-versus-decision distinction maintained, per the programme's standing audit pattern.</p><p><b>3. The consolidated procurement calendar.</b> The track's reads assembled by cadence — the function's operating rhythm:</p><p>1. <b>Daily</b> — the demand screen on its cadence (module 3); the dock's expected arrivals and receipts posted same-day (module 6); invoices entered and matched as they arrive (module 7).<br>2. <b>Weekly</b> — the reorder and consolidation window closing with orders submitted (modules 3, 5); the order book with incoming, overdue, and at-risk (module 5); received-not-billed and billed-not-received (modules 5, 7); open claims and clearing (modules 6-7); the exception queue and held invoices (module 7); the payment run on terms with the verified-account check (module 7); quotation cycles and validity (module 4); quarantine holds (module 6).<br>3. <b>Monthly</b> — buying price changes and completeness (module 2); the price trend read with the selling hand-off (module 2); bank-detail changes (module 1); the amendments read (module 5); the receipt audit and receiving-performance compilation (module 6); statement reconciliations (module 7); blanket consumption (module 5 of this chapter's numbering — Chapter 5); scorecard periods evaluating (Chapters 2-3).<br>4. <b>Quarterly</b> — the trace audit (module 3); response rates, award patterns, and the savings record (module 4); cancellations and closures (module 5); exception patterns (module 7); the settings read; relationship reviews per tier (Chapter 4); the concentration and risk read (module 1).<br>5. <b>Annually</b> — the register lifecycle review (module 1); the planning-field and policy reviews (module 2); re-quote cycles per class and arrangement renewals (module 4, Chapter 5); the scorecard weight review (Chapter 2); the supply-base compilation for the owner (Chapter 4).</p><p><b>4. The calendar's function.</b> Every control in the track is a read someone runs on a date. The calendar assigns each to its owner and cadence, and the function's health is auditable in one question per read: when did it last run, and where are its findings filed.</p>"),
L("Chapter 8 — Worked example: the Emzor year-one review", "13", "<p>This chapter closes the standing case: the first annual review of the arrangement modules 4-7 executed. Values and outcomes are listed per step.</p><p><b>Step 1 — the scorecard compiles (Chapters 2-3).</b> Twelve months of document data evaluate under the 40/35/25 weights:</p><p>1. <b>Price (40)</b> — the band rate held through validity; one notified increase routed and absorbed into the selling review per module 2's hand-off; the trend read shows movement with notice against market reference: the criterion scores in the healthy band.<br>2. <b>Delivery (35)</b> — measured per drop across the year's orders: <b>on-time-in-full at 91%</b> — the misses documented on their orders with the chase-ladder records, none breaking cover into stock-out.<br>3. <b>Quality (25)</b> — the condition record: the year's rejection rate at <b>1.7% of received volume by cause (transit crush)</b>, claims resolved by replacement within agreed deadlines, no inspection failures, resolution conduct prompt. The weighted score lands Emzor in the healthy band, trend stable.</p><p><b>Step 2 — the agenda assembles (Chapter 4).</b> The critical-tier quarterly review, year-end edition: the scorecard and trend; the claims file (all closed, the drop-one replacement's cross-references complete); the exception pattern (the single corrected-invoice pair, closed with documents); payment performance both ways — the business's runs on terms throughout, the terms credibility intact for the negotiation; the commercial pipeline — the arrangement expiring, actual annual volume against the band, and the concentration position with the second manufacturer's share performing.</p><p><b>Step 3 — the conversation runs measured (Chapters 3-4).</b> The review cites periods and documents: the 91% delivery figure with its drop-level records; the damage cause raised with the packaging question (the transit-crush pattern routed to a packing change for the coming year — a specific ask with a criterion and a period); Emzor's own asks heard from their reconciliation file. The minute records the commitments each way.</p><p><b>Step 4 — the renewal prices from evidence (Chapters 4-5).</b> The re-quote calendar owned the expiry: the renewal cycle runs per module 4 with the year's actual volumes as the comparison quantity, the scorecard in the performance columns, and the second manufacturer quoting the overlap. The outcome: the arrangement renews with the band structure adjusted to the measured volume trajectory, the packaging commitment written into terms, and the secondary share confirmed as the standing concentration mitigation. Separately, the consumables classes — stable-demand packing materials — convert from arrangement to <b>blanket order</b> on the consumption evidence: committed volume for a rate inside the band, the trade stated in the decision minute.</p><p><b>Step 5 — the calendar carries it (Chapter 7).</b> The renewal's validity enters the re-quote calendar; the blanket's consumption read starts its monthly cadence; the scorecard rolls into year two measuring the packaging commitment; the next review is dated in the minute.</p><p><b>The track's evidence, complete.</b> One supplier file now demonstrates the whole track: the verified register entry, the sourced rates, the traced demand, the cycle's minutes, the committed order, the signed dock records, the matched invoices, the reconciled statements, the measured scorecard, and the review minute that prices year two — every figure in the conversation answerable to a document in one lookup.</p>"),
L("Chapter 9 — Common errors, the module rule, and the track close", "12", "<p>The errors that recur in supplier management, each with its correction and prevention — then the track's close.</p><p><b>Error 1 — the impression scorecard.</b> Symptom: supplier standing set by the loudest recent event; scores adjusted to justify decided outcomes; the base learning that measurement is negotiable. Correction: scores recomputed from documents. Prevention: criteria computing from document variables, and weights changed only by dated policy decision.</p><p><b>Error 2 — the warning nobody schedules.</b> Symptom: standings fall into warning bands with no conversation following; the restriction gate arriving as a surprise; the improvement route never stated. Correction: the warned suppliers routed to reviews. Prevention: the warning band's action — a scheduled conversation with the criterion named.</p><p><b>Error 3 — the standing exception habit.</b> Symptom: restriction gates overridden routinely for the same suppliers; the ladder's evenness gone; measured consequences replaced by asking. Correction: overrides compiled and routed to the risk review. Prevention: overrides by named role with reasons, and repeated overrides read as concentration findings.</p><p><b>Error 4 — the blanket rollover.</b> Symptom: committed-quantity agreements renewed by inattention at stale rates; consumption never read; shortfalls discovered at expiry. Correction: the renewal re-priced from actuals. Prevention: the monthly consumption read and expiry on the re-quote calendar.</p><p><b>Error 5 — invisible stock at vendors.</b> Symptom: materials at subcontractors untracked or written off informally; consumption variances unworked; the vendor's holdings unreconciled. Correction: the subcontractor warehouse reconciled and variances claimed. Prevention: vendor stock as a warehouse the reads see, on the counting calendar.</p><p><b>Error 6 — the calendar that exists on paper.</b> Symptom: reads defined but unrun; findings unfiled; controls present in the manual and absent in the month. Correction: the calendar re-owned read by read. Prevention: each read with an owner and a date, auditable by the one question — when did it last run, and where are its findings.</p><p><b>Review list.</b> Before the quiz, confirm you can: build scorecards on the 40/35/25 weights from document variables; operate the standings ladder with stated actions and documented overrides; run tiered reviews from assembled agendas to outcome minutes; distinguish blanket orders from arrangements and read consumption; state the subcontracting flow with vendor-warehouse stock and assembled valuation; maintain the Buying Settings register; and run the consolidated calendar.</p><p><b>Module rule 8: the supplier base is managed from documents — performance measured on weighted criteria the papers recorded, standings carrying stated consequences applied evenly, reviews conducted from files to minutes, commitments priced from actual volumes, and every read on a calendar with an owner.</b></p><p><b>The track close.</b> Eight modules built one continuous machine: the verified register (1), the costed item (2), the recorded demand (3), the evidenced rate (4), the committed order (5), the counted arrival (6), the matched claim (7), and the measured relationship (8). The standing case ran through all eight — from Emzor's registration to the 300-carton order, the 177-and-3 dock split, the corrected invoice, and the year-one review that priced year two. The Procurement track is complete.</p>"),
]

